      title: "Emperor of the Romans",
      body: `## The attack on the pope

In April **799**, as Pope **Leo III** rode in a procession through Rome, he was attacked by a band of armed men led by relatives of the previous pope, Hadrian I, who accused Leo of perjury and adultery. They tried to cut out his tongue and gouge out his eyes, and imprisoned him in a monastery. Leo escaped and fled across the Alps to Charlemagne, who was campaigning in Saxony, at Paderborn. Charlemagne received him with honor, and sent him back to Rome with an escort.

In November 800, Charlemagne himself came to Rome to settle the matter. In December, before an assembly of Frankish and Roman clergy and nobles, the pope swore a solemn oath that he was innocent of the charges against him. No one, it was agreed, could judge the pope.

## Christmas Day, 800

Two days later, on **Christmas Day, 800**, Charlemagne attended Mass at **St. Peter's Basilica**. As he rose from prayer before the tomb of St. Peter, Pope Leo placed a crown on his head, and the assembled Romans acclaimed him three times: "To Charles, the most pious Augustus, crowned by God, the great and peace-giving emperor of the Romans, life and victory!" The pope then prostrated himself before the new emperor, in the manner of the Byzantine court. For the first time in more than three centuries, there was an emperor in the West.

## Did Charlemagne want it?

What did the coronation mean, and who planned it? Einhard tells a surprising story: Charlemagne, he says, "at first had such an aversion to it that he declared he would not have set foot in the church that day, though it was a great feast day, if he could have foreseen the design of the pope." Historians have debated this statement endlessly. Was it simple modesty, a literary convention borrowed from the Roman emperors who pretended to refuse power? Did Charlemagne object not to the title but to the way it was conferred—by the pope, as if the pope had the power to make emperors? Or did he fear the reaction of the Byzantines?

What is clear is that the coronation had been discussed at Charlemagne's court for some time. His advisers, including Alcuin, had argued that Charlemagne, who ruled most of the Christian West, protected the pope, and defended the faith, already exercised the authority of an emperor. Moreover, they pointed out, the imperial throne in Constantinople was then occupied by a woman, the empress **Irene**, who had blinded and deposed her own son in 797 to rule alone. Many in the West believed that a woman could not hold the imperial office, and that the throne was therefore vacant.

## Constantinople's reaction

The Byzantines were outraged. For them, there was only one Roman emperor, in Constantinople, the heir of Augustus and Constantine; the coronation of a barbarian king in Rome was a usurpation. For a decade, there was tension and occasional war between the Franks and the Byzantines, especially over Venice and the Adriatic. Finally, in **812**, the Byzantine emperor Michael I recognized Charlemagne as "emperor" (*basileus*), though not as "emperor of the Romans," a title the Byzantines reserved for themselves. The existence of two emperors, one in the East and one in the West, each claiming to be the heir of Rome, would be a source of friction for centuries.

## The meaning of empire

For Charlemagne, the imperial title seems to have meant above all a religious responsibility: to protect the church, spread the faith, and lead the Christian people—the ***populus Christianus***—to salvation. In a letter to Pope Leo III, he described the division of labor between them: "It is our part, with the help of divine piety, to defend by armed strength the holy church of Christ everywhere from the outward onslaught of the pagans and the ravages of the infidels, and to strengthen within it the knowledge of the Catholic faith. It is your part, most holy father, to help our armies with your hands lifted up to God, like Moses."

The coronation also created a precedent that would cause endless conflict: that the pope had the power to make emperors. In the centuries that followed, popes would claim that the imperial crown was theirs to give, and emperors would insist that their authority came directly from God. This conflict would erupt in the eleventh century in the struggle between the papacy and the empire.

## Harun al-Rashid's elephant

Charlemagne's prestige extended far beyond Europe. He exchanged embassies with the Abbasid caliph **Harun al-Rashid** (786–809) in Baghdad, the ruler of the most powerful and sophisticated state of the age, familiar to later readers from the *Thousand and One Nights*. Both rulers had reason to cooperate: Charlemagne wanted protection for Christian pilgrims in Jerusalem, and both were hostile to the Byzantines and to the Umayyads of Spain. In 802, Harun's envoys arrived at Aachen with gifts, including an elaborate water clock that marked the hours with falling bronze balls and mechanical horsemen, and an **elephant** named **Abul-Abbas**, which had traveled from Baghdad by way of North Africa and Italy. The elephant became a sensation, accompanied Charlemagne on campaign, and died in 810. The Frankish annals recorded its death.

## Aachen

In his later years, Charlemagne settled at **Aachen**, in the Rhineland, where he built a palace complex, inspired by Rome, Ravenna, and Constantinople. Its centerpiece, the **Palatine Chapel**, an octagonal domed church modeled on the church of San Vitale in Ravenna, built with columns and marble brought from Rome and Ravenna, still stands as the core of Aachen Cathedral. There, on a simple stone throne in the gallery, Charlemagne sat during services; and there he was buried, after his death on January 28, **814**. For centuries afterward, German kings were crowned at Aachen, on Charlemagne's throne.

> **Pause and consider.** Einhard says Charlemagne would not have entered the church had he known what the pope intended. Why might he have objected to being crowned by the pope?`,
